      "source_version_id": "6f9c1b52-0d34-4a7e-9d21-7c4f5a2b8e10",
      "text": "Table 4.1, measured values for a single resistor. Current 1 A gives 2 V, 2 A gives 4 V and 3 A gives 6 V."
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "6d3925cf-5cfd-507b-9e51-0023faaeafa0",
      "embedding_spec": "gemini-embedding-001:1536",
      "locator": "chapter 4, figure 4.2",
      "name": "figure-4-2",
      "source_version_id": "6f9c1b52-0d34-4a7e-9d21-7c4f5a2b8e10",
      "text": "Figure 4.2 plots voltage in volts against current in amperes; the points lie on a straight line through the origin rising 2 volts per ampere."
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "5c2b2f37-8a99-5f55-b38e-2b1bfb816a16",
      "embedding_spec": "gemini-embedding-001:1536",
      "locator": "chapter 4, section 4.1, paragraph 1",
      "name": "ohms-law-statement",
      "source_version_id": "6f9c1b52-0d34-4a7e-9d21-7c4f5a2b8e10",
      "text": "Ohm's law states that the voltage across a resistor equals the current through it multiplied by its resistance, V = I R."
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "7396810e-386d-56fe-8a91-8830b9bb1991",
      "embedding_spec": "gemini-embedding-001:1536",
      "locator": "chapter 4, section 4.1, paragraph 2",
      "name": "resistance-units",
      "source_version_id": "6f9c1b52-0d34-4a7e-9d21-7c4f5a2b8e10",
      "text": "Resistance is measured in ohms; one ohm is one volt per ampere."
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "e9e9669d-a950-5570-9cd8-09fdb20b2d96",
      "embedding_spec": "gemini-embedding-001:1536",
      "locator": "chapter 4, section 4.3, paragraph 1",
      "name": "power-intro",
      "source_version_id": "6f9c1b52-0d34-4a7e-9d21-7c4f5a2b8e10",
      "text": "Electrical power is the rate at which energy is transferred, measured in watts."
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "8c133af3-9c02-58c3-b582-4eb88279d4b3",
      "embedding_spec": "gemini-embedding-001:1536",
